/*
:name: mem_ordering_program
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: mem_ordering_program
:pyrope_top: mem_ordering_program
*/
// `ordering="program"` showcase (lhdsuite array_problem.md, ruled and
// implemented 2026-07-27). This pair PROVES; it was the fixme tracker that
// drove the implementation and was promoted out of both fixme lists when the
// attribute landed.
//
// PROGRAM (sequential) ordering — the canonical same-cycle sequence:
//   o1 = mem[a1]      // read BEFORE the writes: the OLD stored value,
//                     //   even when a1==a2 or a1==a3
//   mem[a2] = d2
//   mem[a3] = d3      // write-write: if a2==a3 the LAST write (d3) commits
//   o4 = mem[a4]      // read AFTER the writes: sees them, later write
//                     //   winning (a4==a3 -> d3, else a4==a2 -> d2)
//
// Before `ordering` existed the lowering applied ONE per-write-port fwd mask
// to EVERY read port (position-blind), so o1 wrongly forwarded the same-cycle
// writes and a triple collision returned d2 where d3 must win. The `fwd` pin
// is now a per-(read,write) matrix, so o1's row is empty and o4's is full.
// The golden .v encodes the ruled semantics with explicit bypasses on o4 only.
pub mod mem_ordering_program(clk:u1, a1:u2, a2:u2, d2:u2, a3:u2, d3:u2, a4:u2) -> (o1:u2@[], o4:u2@[]) {
  // `= nil` (no initializer) ON PURPOSE: an array init is a RESET VALUE
  // (restored by a one-entry-per-cycle sweep), and this pair is about
  // same-cycle ORDERING only — the golden below declares the array with no
  // power-on contents and no reset, exactly like this.
  reg mem:[4]u2:[ordering="program"]
  o1 = mem[a1]
  mem[a2] = d2
  mem[a3] = d3
  o4 = mem[a4]
}
